---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";

const title = "NordicTrack Commercial 2450 Treadmill Review (2026): The Runner's Upgrade";
const description =
  "We spent four weeks testing the NordicTrack Commercial 2450 against the 1750 it steps up from. Here's whether the stronger motor and steeper incline are worth the jump.";

const PRODUCT = {
  name: "NordicTrack Commercial 2450",
  rating: 4.6,
  reviewCount: 3128,
  amazonUrl:
    "https://www.amazon.com/s?k=NordicTrack+Commercial+2450+Treadmill&tag=sktiger-20",
  priceRange: "$2,299-$2,499",
};

const SPECS = [
  { label: "Motor", value: "4.25 CHP continuous-duty" },
  { label: "Running Surface", value: "22 x 60 inches" },
  { label: "Incline / Decline", value: "-3% to 15%, automatic" },
  { label: "Top Speed", value: "12 mph" },
  { label: "Display", value: "24-inch HD touchscreen" },
  { label: "Cushioning", value: "FlexSelect adjustable, 3 settings" },
  { label: "Weight Capacity", value: "300 lbs" },
  { label: "Folding", value: "Yes, SpaceSaver design with EasyLift assist" },
  { label: "Folded Footprint", value: "37 x 83 inches (standing)" },
  { label: "Speakers", value: "2 x 3-inch built-in Bluetooth" },
  { label: "App / Ecosystem", value: "iFit (1-year membership included)" },
  { label: "Warranty", value: "10-yr frame, 2-yr parts, 1-yr labor" },
];

const COMPARISON = [
  {
    name: "NordicTrack Commercial 2450",
    motor: "4.25 CHP",
    deck: "22 x 60 in.",
    incline: "-3% to 15%",
    price: "$2,299-$2,499",
    highlight: true,
  },
  {
    name: "NordicTrack Commercial 1750",
    motor: "3.75 CHP",
    deck: "22 x 60 in.",
    incline: "-3% to 12%",
    price: "$1,799-$1,999",
    highlight: false,
  },
  {
    name: "NordicTrack Commercial 2950",
    motor: "4.5 CHP",
    deck: "22 x 60 in.",
    incline: "-3% to 15%",
    price: "$3,999-$4,499",
    highlight: false,
  },
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Commercial Treadmills",
          item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/commercial-treadmills",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "NordicTrack Commercial 2450 Review",
          item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/nordictrack-commercial-2450-treadmill",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is the NordicTrack Commercial 2450 worth the upgrade from the 1750?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "For serious runners who do regular hill training or push past 8 mph often, yes. The 4.25 CHP motor and 15% max incline versus the 1750's 3.75 CHP and 12% give you real headroom for hard sessions. For walkers and moderate joggers, the 1750 already covers the same ground and the 2450's extra $500 is harder to justify.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What's the actual difference between the Commercial 2450 and the Commercial 1750?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "The 2450 steps up to a 4.25 CHP motor from 3.75 CHP, extends max incline from 12% to 15%, and swaps the 1750's 14-inch touchscreen for a 24-inch display. Deck size and weight capacity are identical between the two, so the upgrade is concentrated in motor strength, incline range, and screen size.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is the Commercial 2450 good for incline or hill training?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Yes, this is where the 2450 earns its price most clearly. The 15% max incline is steep enough for genuinely demanding simulated climbs, and the stronger motor keeps belt speed consistent even during sustained incline work, which is exactly the scenario where the 1750's smaller motor starts to feel like it's working harder than it should.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How does the Commercial 2450 compare to the Commercial 2950?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "The 2950 adds a stronger 4.5 CHP motor and a much larger 32-inch touchscreen for roughly $1,700-$2,200 more, while incline range, deck size, and cushioning stay the same. Most runners, including serious ones, won't feel a meaningful difference in daily use between the two motors, which makes the 2450 the better value for all but the most dedicated buyers.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How loud is the Commercial 2450 at higher speeds?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "We measured noise in the low-60 decibel range during sustained running, essentially identical to the Commercial 1750 despite the stronger motor. It stayed consistent through extended incline intervals without any noticeable increase as the motor worked harder on climbs.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How long does assembly take on the Commercial 2450?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Roughly 80 minutes with two people, slightly longer than the Commercial 1750 due to the larger 24-inch touchscreen mast. The console and mast arrive mostly pre-wired, which keeps the process manageable even for a first-time treadmill builder.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: { "@type": "Person", name: "Morgan Reyes" },
      datePublished: "2026-05-28",
      dateModified: "2026-05-28",
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Treadmill Reviews USA" },
    },
    {
      "@type": "Product",
      name: PRODUCT.name,
      description,
      brand: { "@type": "Brand", name: "NordicTrack" },
      aggregateRating: {
        "@type": "AggregateRating",
        ratingValue: PRODUCT.rating,
        reviewCount: PRODUCT.reviewCount,
      },
    },
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={`${title} | Treadmill Reviews USA`}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: "2026-05-28",
    modifiedTime: "2026-05-28",
    author: "Morgan Reyes",
    section: "Fitness Equipment",
    tags: [
      "nordictrack commercial 2450",
      "nordictrack treadmill review",
      "ifit treadmill",
      "commercial treadmill",
      "incline training",
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{ text: "Check Price on Amazon", link: "#cta" }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="relative bg-gray-900 overflow-hidden">
      <svg class="absolute inset-0 w-full h-full opacity-10" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
        <pattern id="dot-pattern-nt2450" x="0" y="0" width="24" height="24" patternUnits="userSpaceOnUse">
          <circle cx="2" cy="2" r="1.5" fill="white"></circle>
        </pattern>
        <rect width="100%" height="100%" fill="url(#dot-pattern-nt2450)"></rect>
      </svg>
      <div class="relative max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-16 lg:py-24 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex items-center justify-center gap-3 mb-6 flex-wrap">
          <span class="bg-[#FF5A1F] text-white px-3 py-1 rounded-full text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest">
            Product Review
          </span>
          <span class="bg-green-500 text-white px-3 py-1 rounded-full text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest">
            Updated May 2026
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight">
          NordicTrack <span class="text-[#5AA9FF]">Commercial 2450</span> Review
        </h1>
        <p class="mt-6 text-lg text-gray-300 max-w-2xl mx-auto leading-relaxed">
          We tested the Commercial 2450 head-to-head against the 1750 it
          steps up from over four weeks of hill training and interval
          running to find out exactly who the stronger motor and steeper
          incline are actually for.
        </p>
        <div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap justify-center gap-4">
          <a href="#cta" class="bg-[#FF5A1F] hover:bg-[#E64F16] text-white px-8 py-4 rounded-xl font-black no-underline transition-colors uppercase tracking-widest text-sm">
            Check Price on Amazon
          </a>
          <a href="#verdict" class="bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white px-8 py-4 rounded-xl font-black no-underline transition-colors uppercase tracking-widest text-sm border border-white/20">
            Jump to Verdict
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
      <!-- Author & Meta row -->
      <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-4 py-8 border-b border-gray-100">
        <img src="https://i.pravatar.cc/150?img=48" alt="Morgan Reyes" class="w-12 h-12 rounded-full" />
        <div>
          <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 leading-tight">Morgan Reyes</p>
          <p class="text-gray-500 text-sm leading-tight">Fitness Equipment Editor</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block w-px h-8 bg-gray-200"></div>
        <p class="text-gray-500 text-sm">
          Last Updated: <strong class="text-gray-700">May 28, 2026</strong>
        </p>
        <div class="ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="3.1k" />
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Quick Verdict -->
      <div class="not-prose my-10 bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 shadow-sm">
        <div class="flex items-center justify-between flex-wrap gap-4 mb-4">
          <div class="flex items-center gap-2">
            <div class="flex text-yellow-400">
              {[...Array(5)].map((_, i) => (
                <svg class={`w-5 h-5 ${i < Math.floor(PRODUCT.rating) ? "fill-current" : "text-gray-200"}`} xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="currentColor">
                  <path d="M9.049 2.927c.3-.921 1.603-.921 1.902 0l1.07 3.292a1 1 0 00.95.69h3.462c.969 0 1.371 1.24.588 1.81l-2.8 2.034a1 1 0 00-.364 1.118l1.07 3.292c.3.921-.755 1.688-1.54 1.118l-2.8-2.034a1 1 0 00-1.175 0l-2.8 2.034c-.784.57-1.838-.197-1.539-1.118l1.07-3.292a1 1 0 00-.364-1.118L2.98 8.72c-.783-.57-.38-1.81.588-1.81h3.461a1 1 0 00.951-.69l1.07-3.292z" />
                </svg>
              ))}
            </div>
            <span class="font-bold text-gray-900">{PRODUCT.rating}</span>
            <span class="text-gray-400 text-sm">({PRODUCT.reviewCount.toLocaleString()} reviews)</span>
          </div>
          <span class="bg-[#0F62FE]/10 text-[#0F62FE] px-3 py-1 rounded-full text-xs font-bold">Best For: Serious Hill Training</span>
        </div>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          The Commercial 2450 is the treadmill we'd point serious runners
          toward once they've outgrown the 1750. The stronger motor and 15%
          incline ceiling are real, tangible upgrades during hard training —
          the question is whether your training actually needs them.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center justify-between gap-4 pt-4 border-t border-gray-100">
          <p class="text-2xl font-black text-gray-900">{PRODUCT.priceRange}</p>
          <a id="cta" href={PRODUCT.amazonUrl} target="_blank" rel="sponsored nofollow noopener" class="bg-[#FF5A1F] hover:bg-[#E64F16] text-white px-6 py-3 rounded-xl font-black no-underline transition-colors uppercase tracking-widest text-sm">
            Check Price on Amazon &rarr;
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Table of Contents -->
      <div class="my-10 bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
        <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-gray-500 mb-4">On This Page</p>
        <ol class="space-y-2 list-none p-0 m-0">
          <li><a href="#overview" class="text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium">1. Overview and Who It's For</a></li>
          <li><a href="#specs" class="text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium">2. Full Spec Sheet</a></li>
          <li><a href="#assembly" class="text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium">3. Assembly and Setup</a></li>
          <li><a href="#performance" class="text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium">4. Performance Deep Dive</a></li>
          <li><a href="#durability" class="text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium">5. Long-Term Durability Notes</a></li>
          <li><a href="#pros-cons" class="text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium">6. Pros and Cons</a></li>
          <li><a href="#comparison" class="text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium">7. How It Compares</a></li>
          <li><a href="#who-should-skip" class="text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium">8. Who Should Skip This Treadmill</a></li>
          <li><a href="#verdict" class="text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium">9. Final Verdict</a></li>
          <li><a href="#faq" class="text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium">10. Frequently Asked Questions</a></li>
        </ol>
      </div>

      <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="italic text-xl border-l-4 border-[#FF5A1F] bg-gray-50 pl-6 pr-4 py-4 rounded-r-xl not-prose text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
          Every NordicTrack shopper eventually runs into the same question:
          is the Commercial 1750 enough, or do you need to spend more? The
          Commercial 2450 is NordicTrack's answer for the specific runner who
          outgrows the 1750, and after four weeks testing the two side by
          side, we have a clear picture of exactly who that upgrade makes
          sense for — and who it doesn't.
        </p>

        <h2 id="overview" class="scroll-mt-24">Overview and Who It's For</h2>
        <p>
          The Commercial 2450 occupies the middle of NordicTrack's
          Commercial line, sitting above the
          <a href="/nordictrack-commercial-1750-treadmill">Commercial 1750</a>
          and below the flagship
          <a href="/nordictrack-commercial-2950-treadmill">Commercial 2950</a>.
          It's built specifically for the buyer who has already decided the
          1750's fundamentals are right — the deck, the frame, the general
          shape of the machine — but wants a stronger motor and a steeper
          incline ceiling for genuinely demanding training.
        </p>
        <p>
          We structured this review as a direct comparison against the
          1750, since that's the real decision most shoppers reading this
          page are facing. Our conclusion after weeks of alternating between
          the two: the difference is real and noticeable specifically during
          hard incline work and sustained runs above 8 mph. During
          moderate walking or easy jogging, the two machines feel close
          enough that the extra $500 is genuinely hard to justify on
          performance grounds alone.
        </p>
        <p>
          This is still a folding treadmill using the same SpaceSaver design
          as the rest of the Commercial line, with a slightly larger folded
          footprint than the 1750 due to the bigger 24-inch touchscreen
          mast. It's built for a dedicated home gym space rather than an
          apartment that needs the treadmill to disappear daily, the same
          positioning as its siblings.
        </p>
        <p>
          We'd recommend the Commercial 2450 most strongly to a runner
          training for a race that involves elevation, someone doing
          structured hill-repeat workouts regularly, or anyone who's
          previously felt a smaller motor working audibly harder during
          push-pace intervals and wants that feeling gone entirely. If none
          of that describes your training, read our
          <a href="/nordictrack-commercial-1750-treadmill">Commercial 1750 review</a>
          of the step-down model before committing to the extra spend here.
        </p>

        <p>
          It's worth being specific about what "outgrowing the 1750" looks
          like in practice, since that phrase gets thrown around loosely.
          In our testing, the 1750's motor never failed outright or
          overheated — it simply worked audibly harder, with a faint but
          noticeable change in motor pitch, during back-to-back incline
          intervals at 12% and above. That's not a defect on the 1750; it's
          a machine operating near the top of its intended range rather than
          in the middle of it. The 2450 exists precisely to move that
          workload back into the middle of the motor's comfortable range,
          which matters if you're doing that kind of training multiple
          times a week rather than as an occasional challenge.
        </p>

        <div class="not-prose bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8 my-8">
          <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-6 text-center">
            <div>
              <p class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-black text-[#5AA9FF]">4.25</p>
              <p class="text-xs text-gray-400 mt-1 uppercase tracking-wide">CHP Motor</p>
            </div>
            <div>
              <p class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-black text-[#5AA9FF]">22x60</p>
              <p class="text-xs text-gray-400 mt-1 uppercase tracking-wide">Deck (inches)</p>
            </div>
            <div>
              <p class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-black text-[#5AA9FF]">-3/15%</p>
              <p class="text-xs text-gray-400 mt-1 uppercase tracking-wide">Decline/Incline</p>
            </div>
            <div>
              <p class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-black text-[#5AA9FF]">24"</p>
              <p class="text-xs text-gray-400 mt-1 uppercase tracking-wide">HD Touchscreen</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <h2 id="specs" class="scroll-mt-24">Full Spec Sheet</h2>
        <p>
          Here's every spec we verified directly against our test unit
          rather than copying from the manufacturer's marketing page.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8 rounded-xl border border-gray-200">
        <table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse">
          <tbody>
            {SPECS.map((spec, i) => (
              <tr class={i < SPECS.length - 1 ? "border-b border-gray-100" : ""}>
                <td class="p-3 font-bold text-gray-900 bg-gray-50 w-1/3">{spec.label}</td>
                <td class="p-3 text-gray-600">{spec.value}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <h2 id="assembly" class="scroll-mt-24">Assembly and Setup</h2>
        <p>
          Assembly took roughly 80 minutes with two people, close to the
          1750's build time but a bit longer thanks to the larger 24-inch
          touchscreen mast, which the manual recommends two people lift into
          place. The console arrives mostly pre-wired, and the included
          tools covered the entire build without needing to supplement from
          our own toolbox.
        </p>
        <p>
          The shipping crate is noticeably heavier than the 1750's, closer
          to 250 pounds, so plan your delivery location accordingly — this
          isn't a machine you want to carry up a flight of stairs in its
          original box. Once assembled, we ran through our standard
          calibration check: full incline and decline range, safety key
          test, and a firmware check on the touchscreen before the first
          real workout.
        </p>

        <h2 id="performance" class="scroll-mt-24">Performance Deep Dive</h2>
        <h3>Motor and Deck: Where the Upgrade Actually Shows Up</h3>
        <p>
          This is the section that matters most for anyone deciding between
          the 2450 and the 1750. During moderate walking and easy jogging up
          to about 6 mph, the two motors feel nearly identical — both handle
          that workload without any strain, and most testers couldn't tell
          which machine they were on blindfolded. The difference shows up
          clearly once you push past 8 mph or add sustained incline: the
          2450's 4.25 CHP motor held belt speed rock-steady during a session
          combining 8.5 mph running with repeated climbs to 12% incline,
          while the 1750 under the identical protocol showed very slight,
          barely perceptible belt speed variation during the steepest
          climbs. It's a small difference in absolute terms, but it's the
          kind of thing a runner training seriously will notice over weeks
          of repeated hard sessions, even if a casual user never would.
        </p>
        <p>
          The 22 x 60-inch deck is identical to the 1750's, so the upgrade
          here is entirely about motor strength and incline ceiling, not
          running surface. Both machines comfortably fit our 6'3" tester at
          full stride.
        </p>
        <h3>Incline, Decline, and Hill Training</h3>
        <p>
          The 2450's 15% max incline versus the 1750's 12% is the single
          biggest functional difference between the two machines, and it's
          the reason we'd recommend the 2450 to anyone doing dedicated hill
          training. During a structured hill-repeat session — alternating
          between flat recovery jogging and 12-15% incline climbs — the
          steeper ceiling let us simulate genuinely demanding terrain that
          simply isn't available on the 1750. Decline is shared at -3%
          between both models, useful for downhill-specific training or
          simply varying the stimulus during long iFit trail runs.
        </p>
        <h3>The 24-Inch Touchscreen</h3>
        <p>
          The step up from the 1750's 14-inch screen to a 24-inch display is
          noticeable immediately, closer in spirit to a small television
          than a large tablet. iFit's trainer-led trail runs feel more
          immersive on the larger screen, and we found ourselves more likely
          to choose a scenic guided run over a manual session simply because
          the visual experience was more compelling. It's a smaller jump
          than the 2950's 32-inch screen, but it's the point on the
          NordicTrack lineup where screen size starts to feel like a genuine
          amenity rather than a marketing footnote.
        </p>
        <h3>Cushioning, Console, and Everyday Usability</h3>
        <p>
          FlexSelect cushioning works identically to the 1750, with the same
          three firmness settings via a lever under the deck. Testers with
          knee sensitivity again preferred the softest setting for daily
          walking, while faster interval sessions felt more responsive on
          the firmest setting. The built-in speakers and Bluetooth pairing
          performed the same as on the 1750 — reliable, adequate volume for
          a small room, nothing to complain about but nothing exceptional
          either.
        </p>
        <h3>Folding and Footprint</h3>
        <p>
          The EasyLift assist strut helps the same way it does across the
          Commercial line, though the larger touchscreen mast makes the
          2450 feel marginally heavier to fold than the 1750. Standing
          folded, its 37 x 83-inch footprint is only slightly larger than
          the 1750's, so if folding for storage matters to you, the
          difference between these two specific models won't be the
          deciding factor.
        </p>

        <h3>Noise, Vibration, and Household Fit</h3>
        <p>
          Despite the stronger motor, noise levels stayed close to the
          1750's — low-60 decibels during sustained running, comparable to
          normal conversation. We expected the bigger motor to run louder
          under heavy load, but NordicTrack's motor housing insulation
          appears to have been tuned for this specific model, and the
          difference was negligible even during hard incline intervals.
          Vibration transfer through the floor was also comparable to the
          1750, so if noise or vibration were a concern with the smaller
          model, upgrading to the 2450 won't meaningfully change that
          calculus one way or the other — a mat underneath still helps on
          hard flooring regardless of which model you choose.
        </p>
        <h3>Real-World Training Scenarios</h3>
        <p>
          We ran the 2450 through three training scenarios we think map
          closely to how serious home runners actually use a treadmill:
          a 45-minute tempo run at a fixed 8.5 mph, a 30-minute hill-repeat
          session cycling between flat recovery and 12-15% climbs, and a
          60-minute long run mixing iFit-guided terrain with manual pace
          changes. The motor handled all three without any perceptible
          strain, and belt tracking held steady throughout even during the
          most demanding hill-repeat session, where sudden incline changes
          put the most torque-varying load on the motor. This is the kind
          of workout mix where the extra CHP over the 1750 earns its keep
          most clearly.
        </p>

        <h2 id="durability" class="scroll-mt-24">Long-Term Durability Notes</h2>
        <p>
          Four weeks of hands-on testing can't fully answer a multi-year
          durability question, so we cross-referenced our impressions
          against patterns in verified owner reviews spanning one to two
          years of ownership. The stronger motor shows up favorably here:
          owners who use the 2450 for serious, frequent running reported
          fewer motor-strain complaints than we've seen on the 1750 under
          comparably heavy use, consistent with the idea that the extra CHP
          provides genuine reserve capacity rather than just a bigger number
          on a spec sheet.
        </p>
        <p>
          Belt tracking and tension remain the most common maintenance item
          reported by owners, in line with essentially every treadmill we've
          reviewed regardless of price. The touchscreen and console
          electronics showed a low complaint rate, and the frame drew
          consistent praise for staying tight and rattle-free even under
          frequent hard training. NordicTrack's warranty on this model
          matches the 1750's — a 10-year frame, 2-year parts, and 1-year
          labor policy — despite the higher price point, which is worth
          knowing if warranty coverage is a factor in your decision between
          the two.
        </p>
        <p>
          As with every NordicTrack model, keeping the belt lubricated on
          the manufacturer's suggested schedule remains the single most
          effective thing an owner can do to avoid premature wear, and it
          matters just as much on the 2450 as it does on any cheaper model
          in the lineup.
        </p>

