        <h2 id="who-should-skip" class="scroll-mt-24">Who Should Skip This Treadmill</h2>
        <p>
          It's also worth flagging a subtler group who should skip the
          2450: shoppers who are buying based on a spec sheet comparison
          alone rather than their actual training history. It's easy to
          look at "4.25 CHP versus 3.75 CHP" and assume more is always
          better, but the honest truth from our testing is that the
          difference is imperceptible unless you're regularly pushing pace
          or incline hard enough to feel the 1750 working near its limit.
          If you've never owned a treadmill before and don't yet know your
          own training habits, we'd suggest starting with the 1750 and
          upgrading later if you find yourself wanting more — it's a far
          cheaper mistake to correct than the reverse.
        </p>
        <p>
          If your training is mostly flat-ground walking or easy jogging,
          you will not feel the difference between this machine and the
          <a href="/nordictrack-commercial-1750-treadmill">Commercial 1750</a>,
          and the extra $500 is better spent elsewhere or simply saved. If
          you're a serious runner already leaning toward the strongest
          possible motor and largest possible screen regardless of price,
          skip straight to the <a href="/nordictrack-commercial-2950-treadmill">Commercial 2950</a>
          rather than treating the 2450 as a stepping stone you'll want to
          upgrade from again later. And if you're still comparing broadly
          across budgets, our <a href="/best-treadmill-for-home-reviews">best treadmills for home</a>
          guide is a better starting point than committing to a specific
          NordicTrack tier this early in your research.
        </p>

        <p>
          One more consideration worth weighing: financing and resale.
          Because the 2450 sits at a genuinely popular mid-flagship price
          point, it tends to hold resale value slightly better than either
          the entry-level 1750 or the far pricier 2950, both of which see
          steeper depreciation curves in the used market — the 1750 because
          it's the highest-volume model with the most secondhand supply, and
          the 2950 because its price ceiling narrows the pool of buyers
          willing to pay used-market prices for a flagship. If resale value
          factors into your decision at all, that's a small point in the
          2450's favor.
        </p>

        <h2 id="verdict" class="scroll-mt-24">Final Verdict</h2>
        <p>
          The NordicTrack Commercial 2450 does exactly what it sets out to
          do: it takes everything that already works about the Commercial
          1750 and adds real, usable headroom for runners who need it. The
          stronger motor and steeper incline aren't marketing dressing —
          during hard sessions, we could feel the difference directly, and
          owners doing serious hill training will feel it too.
        </p>
        <p>
          The honest caveat is the same one we'd give about any mid-tier
          upgrade: buy this because your training demands it, not because
          it's the next model up from the one you were already considering.
          For walkers and easy joggers, the 1750 remains the smarter buy.
          For runners who regularly push pace and incline, the 2450 is worth
          every one of the extra dollars.
        </p>
        <p>
          If you're still on the fence after reading this far, our practical
          advice is to think back over your last few months of actual
          training rather than your aspirational training plan. Runners
          consistently underestimate how much of their weekly mileage is
          genuinely easy-paced, flat-ground running, which the 1750 handles
          just as well as the 2450 does. Buy the upgrade for the training
          you're actually doing today, not the training you might do
          someday — you can always trade up later once your mileage and
          incline demands catch up to the machine.
        </p>
